# Trellis.Microservices Sample — REST client requests
#
# Works with:
#   - VS Code: "REST Client" extension (humao.rest-client)
#   - Visual Studio 2022 17.8+: built-in .http file support
#   - JetBrains Rider / IntelliJ: built-in HTTP client
#
# Prereq: Aspire AppHost running.
#   dotnet run --project examples/Sample/Sample.AppHost
#
# This boots Gateway (port 5001) + Orders + Billing and opens the Aspire dashboard
# (https://localhost:17051 by default). Logs, traces, and metrics from all three
# services flow into the dashboard so you can SEE the gateway -> orders / billing
# trace span fan-out in real time.
#
# See examples/Sample/README.md for the full walkthrough.

@host = http://localhost:5001

### 1. Orders — happy path
# Sends an X-Test-Actor JSON envelope to the Gateway. The Gateway's
# DevelopmentActorProvider parses it, AddTrellisActorForwarding mints a fresh JWT
# (audience="orders" because YARP matched the "orders-route" cluster) and YARP
# forwards to the Orders service. Orders validates via OIDC discovery (fetches
# JWKS from Gateway), hydrates the Actor via TrellisInternalJwtActorProvider,
# and echoes it back. Open the Aspire dashboard's Traces tab to see the
# Gateway -> Orders span.
#
# Expected: 200 OK + "service":"orders" + round-tripped Actor.
GET {{host}}/api/orders
X-Test-Actor: {"Id":"alice","Permissions":["orders:read","orders:write"],"ForbiddenPermissions":["orders:delete"],"Attributes":{"tenant_id":"acme-corp"}}

### 2. Billing — happy path (SAME actor, DIFFERENT route)
# Same X-Test-Actor envelope, different URL prefix. YARP matches the
# "billing-route" cluster, AddTrellisActorForwarding mints a SEPARATE JWT with
# audience="billing", forwards to the Billing service. Billing's JwtBearer pins
# audience="billing" so it accepts; the token minted for /api/orders would NOT
# validate here (per-cluster audience pinning — see scenario 6).
#
# Expected: 200 OK + "service":"billing" + same Actor as scenario 1.
GET {{host}}/api/billing
X-Test-Actor: {"Id":"alice","Permissions":["orders:read","billing:read"],"ForbiddenPermissions":[],"Attributes":{"tenant_id":"acme-corp"}}

### 3. Orders — different tenant (carol / contoso)
# Proves the tenant_id ABAC claim flows through to Actor.Attributes on the
# downstream side.
GET {{host}}/api/orders
X-Test-Actor: {"Id":"carol","Permissions":["orders:read"],"ForbiddenPermissions":[],"Attributes":{"tenant_id":"contoso"}}

### 4. Missing tenant_id — fail-closed via RequiredAttributes
# Expected: 401 Unauthorized. The downstream Orders service's
# TrellisInternalJwtActorProvider has RequiredAttributes = ["tenant_id"] so a
# request without a tenant claim is rejected before the endpoint runs. This is
# the framework's defense against a downstream service ever evaluating a request
# without knowing which tenant it belongs to.
GET {{host}}/api/orders
X-Test-Actor: {"Id":"bob","Permissions":["orders:read"],"ForbiddenPermissions":[],"Attributes":{}}

### 5. No actor header at all
# Expected: 401 Unauthorized. DevelopmentActorProvider falls back to the default
# actor ("guest" with no permissions/attributes); without tenant_id the downstream
# fails closed.
GET {{host}}/api/orders

### 6. Unknown route — YARP no-match
# Expected: 404 Not Found. Proves YARP is actually doing path-based routing —
# only /api/orders/{**catch-all} and /api/billing/{**catch-all} are mapped.
GET {{host}}/api/unknown
X-Test-Actor: {"Id":"alice","Permissions":[],"ForbiddenPermissions":[],"Attributes":{"tenant_id":"acme"}}

### 7. OIDC discovery document (published by Gateway via MapTrellisDiscoveryEndpoint)
# This is what the downstream services' AddJwtBearer(o.Authority = "http://localhost:5001")
# auto-fetches to learn where the JWKS lives.
GET {{host}}/.well-known/openid-configuration

### 8. JWKS document (the Gateway's public signing keys)
# Downstream services fetch this once at startup, cache it, and use it to verify
# every inbound JWT's signature. The "kid" claim on the JWT picks the matching key.
GET {{host}}/.well-known/jwks.json
